# =============================================================================
# Line endings
#
# THIS FILE EXISTS BECAUSE OF A REAL TRAP, NOT AS BOILERPLATE.
#
# This repository is developed on Windows with `core.autocrlf=true`, which
# rewrites LF to CRLF on checkout. That is harmless for TypeScript and fatal
# for files a Linux kernel or a container runtime has to parse:
#
#   * a shell script with CRLF fails with "bad interpreter: /bin/sh^M";
#   * a Dockerfile with CRLF can carry a stray \r into ENV values and into
#     RUN commands, producing errors that name a line which looks correct.
#
# The failure appears only in the container, never on the machine that wrote
# the file, which is the worst place for it to appear.
#
# `text eol=lf` forces LF in the working tree regardless of the local
# `autocrlf` setting, so this holds for anyone who clones the repository — it
# is not a per-developer configuration that has to be remembered.
# =============================================================================

# Default: let git normalise text, and detect binary automatically.
* text=auto

# Anything Linux parses directly.
Dockerfile      text eol=lf
*.dockerfile    text eol=lf
.dockerignore   text eol=lf
*.sh            text eol=lf
*.bash          text eol=lf

# SQL, for consistency only.
#
# Checked rather than assumed: migration checksums are NOT at risk here.
# packages/db/scripts/migrate.ts normalises CRLF to LF before it hashes the
# file, so a line-ending change cannot alter a checksum or trip the
# immutability guard on an already-applied migration. Written down because
# the opposite is the obvious assumption, and believing it would mean never
# touching these files again.
*.sql           text eol=lf

# Lockfile: never normalise. A rewritten lockfile is a diff nobody can review.
pnpm-lock.yaml  -text

# Binary assets, so git never tries to normalise them.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
